[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":37},"en/routes/conteudo-do-cubo.tsx",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10,"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-7,"_20":21,"_22":-7,"_23":24,"_25":26},"tipo","etapa","cubo",{"_11":35,"_13":35,"_28":36},"id","basico-antes","nome","Before you start","rascunho",false,"html","<p>The <a href=\"/en/glossary#basico\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Most people's first method: it solves the cube layer by layer, in 8 steps, with few algorithms. It's slow, but it teaches how the pieces…\">beginner method</a> solves the cube in layers: first the bottom one, then the middle one and last the top one. There are 8 short steps, and each one uses at most one or two algorithms. Read this step with the cube in your hand.</p>\n<h2>The pieces</h2>\n<p>The cube has three kinds of piece:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Centers</strong> (6): they have a single color and never leave their place. The center tells you the color of that face: if the front center is green, the front face will end up all green.</li>\n<li><strong>Edges</strong> (12): they sit between two centers and have two colors.</li>\n<li><strong>Corners</strong> (8): they sit at the tips and have three colors.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"diagramas\"><figure class=\"diagrama\" data-cubo=\"{&quot;cubo&quot;:&quot;3x3&quot;,&quot;embaralhar&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;tira&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;modo&quot;:&quot;centers&quot;}\"><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/5902e0dad71dbc18.svg\" alt=\"The centers: one color, always in the same place.\" width=\"140\" height=\"140\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The centers: one color, always in the same place.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"diagrama\" data-cubo=\"{&quot;cubo&quot;:&quot;3x3&quot;,&quot;embaralhar&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;tira&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;modo&quot;:&quot;edges&quot;}\"><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/b19c3503933c06b6.svg\" alt=\"The edges: two colors.\" width=\"140\" height=\"140\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The edges: two colors.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"diagrama\" data-cubo=\"{&quot;cubo&quot;:&quot;3x3&quot;,&quot;embaralhar&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;tira&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;modo&quot;:&quot;corners&quot;}\"><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/622fda94fd4126dc.svg\" alt=\"The corners: three colors.\" width=\"140\" height=\"140\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The corners: three colors.</figcaption></figure></div>\n<p>The colors always come in opposite <a href=\"/en/glossary#par\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A corner and an edge that go together into the same slot in F2L.\">pairs</a>: <strong>white and yellow</strong>, <strong>green and blue</strong>, <strong>red and orange</strong>. If white is on the bottom, yellow is on top.</p>\n<p>A white and green edge has only one right place: between the white center and the green center. A white, green and orange corner goes between those three centers. Solving the cube is taking each piece to its place, with the colors facing the right way.</p>\n<h2>How to hold the cube</h2>\n<div class=\"diagramas\"><figure class=\"diagrama\" data-cubo=\"{&quot;cubo&quot;:&quot;3x3&quot;,&quot;embaralhar&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;tira&quot;:&quot;&quot;}\"><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/8e8b8e3af0262584.svg\" alt=\"The solved cube, in the site's position: yellow on top, green in front.\" width=\"140\" height=\"140\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The solved cube, in the site&#39;s position: yellow on top, green in front.</figcaption></figure></div>\n<p>Throughout the method, hold the cube with <strong>yellow on top</strong> and <strong>white on the bottom</strong>. Start with green in front. During a step, you turn the whole cube to work on another face, but yellow stays on top.</p>\n<p>This is the position of the whole site: the diagrams, the algorithms and the cards start from it. To <a href=\"/en/glossary#embaralhamento\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"A sequence of moves that mixes up the cube before solving. In competitions, everyone gets the same one.\">scramble</a>, the competition rule is different (white on top and green in front). After scrambling, turn the cube so yellow is on top.</p>\n<h2>How to read an algorithm</h2>\n<p>Each letter is a face, turned as seen looking straight at it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>R</code> is the right, <code>L</code> the left, <code>U</code> the top, <code>D</code> the bottom, <code>F</code> the front and <code>B</code> the back.</li>\n<li>The letter alone is a quarter turn clockwise, <strong>as seen looking at the face that turns</strong>.</li>\n<li>With an apostrophe (<code>R&#39;</code>), it goes counterclockwise.</li>\n<li>With a 2 (<code>R2</code>), it is a half turn.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"diagramas\"><figure class=\"diagrama tira\" data-cubo=\"{&quot;cubo&quot;:&quot;3x3&quot;,&quot;embaralhar&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;tira&quot;:&quot;R&quot;}\"><ol aria-label=\"R\"><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/535087fda63c8529-0.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"giro\"><code>R</code><span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></span></li><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/535087fda63c8529-1.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"></li></ol><figcaption><code>R</code>: the right side goes up.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"diagrama tira\" data-cubo=\"{&quot;cubo&quot;:&quot;3x3&quot;,&quot;embaralhar&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;tira&quot;:&quot;U'&quot;}\"><ol aria-label=\"U'\"><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/98fc51c1d6698d3c-0.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"giro\"><code>U'</code><span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></span></li><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/98fc51c1d6698d3c-1.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"></li></ol><figcaption><code>U&#39;</code>: the top goes to the right.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"diagrama tira\" data-cubo=\"{&quot;cubo&quot;:&quot;3x3&quot;,&quot;embaralhar&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;tira&quot;:&quot;F&quot;}\"><ol aria-label=\"F\"><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/ac9dc0e9cf5c1f2f-0.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"giro\"><code>F</code><span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></span></li><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/ac9dc0e9cf5c1f2f-1.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"></li></ol><figcaption><code>F</code>: the front turns like a clock.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"diagrama tira\" data-cubo=\"{&quot;cubo&quot;:&quot;3x3&quot;,&quot;embaralhar&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;tira&quot;:&quot;L&quot;}\"><ol aria-label=\"L\"><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/33f4ae00625684b9-0.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"giro\"><code>L</code><span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></span></li><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/33f4ae00625684b9-1.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"></li></ol><figcaption><code>L</code>: the left side goes down.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"diagrama tira\" data-cubo=\"{&quot;cubo&quot;:&quot;3x3&quot;,&quot;embaralhar&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;tira&quot;:&quot;D&quot;}\"><ol aria-label=\"D\"><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/c00e6b770d058703-0.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"giro\"><code>D</code><span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></span></li><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/c00e6b770d058703-1.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"></li></ol><figcaption><code>D</code>: the bottom goes to the right.</figcaption></figure></div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Watch out:</strong> <code>L</code>, <code>D</code> and <code>B</code> are on the sides you can&#39;t see. Their clock is as seen looking at them, so from the front they seem to turn the other way: in <code>L</code> the left side goes down, in <code>D</code> the bottom layer goes to the right. When in doubt, turn the cube to look at the face, turn it and turn the cube back. The <a href=\"/en/3x3/notation\">notation</a> shows all of them.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The algorithm that shows up most in the method is <code>R U R&#39; U&#39;</code>. It is called the <strong><a href=\"/en/glossary#sexy-move\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The trigger R U R' U', the most common of all.\">sexy move</a></strong>: the right side goes up, the top turns to the left, the right side goes down and the top comes back. Repeat it a few times until it comes without thinking.</p>\n<div class=\"diagramas\"><figure class=\"diagrama tira\" data-cubo=\"{&quot;cubo&quot;:&quot;3x3&quot;,&quot;embaralhar&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;tira&quot;:&quot;R U R' U'&quot;}\"><ol aria-label=\"R U R' U'\"><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/0f535620d87908e1-0.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"giro\"><code>R</code><span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></span></li><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/0f535620d87908e1-1.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"giro\"><code>U</code><span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></span></li><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/0f535620d87908e1-2.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"giro\"><code>R'</code><span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></span></li><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/0f535620d87908e1-3.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"><span class=\"giro\"><code>U'</code><span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></span></li><li><img src=\"/imagens/diagramas/0f535620d87908e1-4.svg\" alt=\"\" width=\"96\" height=\"96\" loading=\"lazy\"></li></ol><figcaption>The sexy move, one move at a time. The last diagram is the cube at the end of it.</figcaption></figure></div>\n<p>All the moves are on the <a href=\"/en/3x3/notation\">notation</a> page, with a diagram for each one.</p>\n<h2>How to use this guide</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> the diagrams are examples. Your cube will have other colors out of place, and that&#39;s fine: in each step, look only at the pieces of that step (the colored ones in the diagram) and ignore the rest.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Do one step at a time, until you can do it without looking. Then scramble (the site&#39;s <a href=\"/en/3x3/scrambles\">scrambles</a> help) and solve from the start as far as you know. When every step comes without looking, several times in a row, you have solved the cube on your own.</p>\n","treino","metodos",[30],"anterior","proximo",{"_13":27,"_28":29},"grupos",[],"The daisy","endereco","/3x3/basico/flor",{"_13":31,"_32":33,"_28":34},"Beginner method","numero",1,"/3x3/basico#antes","3x3","/3x3/",{"_38":39,"_40":41},"cubos",[44,45,46,47,48,49],"idiomas",[42,43],"pt","en",{"_11":35,"_13":35},{"_11":56,"_13":56},{"_11":55,"_13":55},{"_11":54,"_13":54},{"_11":52,"_13":53},{"_11":50,"_13":51},"pyraminx","Pyraminx","megaminx","Megaminx","5x5","4x4","2x2"]
